WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.400
The world's

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.434
longest scientific

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.502
study of happiness

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:04.504
is now in its 85th year.

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:06.406
Researchers at Harvard have been studying

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:07.607
a pool of subjects

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:09.909
and their descendants since 1938.

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:11.411
The objective

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:12.412
to unlock the age

00:00:12.412 --> 00:00:14.714
old question of what makes a good life.

00:00:14.748 --> 00:00:17.784
Originally there were 724 participants.

00:00:18.118 --> 00:00:20.453
268 were sophomores at Harvard.

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:21.955
Their ranks included

00:00:21.955 --> 00:00:22.655
future President

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:23.857
John F Kennedy and future

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:26.526
Washington Post editor Ben Bradlee.

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:27.327
The study later

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:28.862
incorporated a parallel one

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:29.429
that was tracking

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:32.432
456 less privileged inner city

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:33.800
Boston boys.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.535
Thereafter, the pool was expanded

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:38.071
to include 1300 descendants.

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:39.205
They've been tracking these folks

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:40.540
for 84 years

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:41.908
and the retention rate

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:43.743
for the original participants

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:46.046
an amazing 84%.

00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:46.980
Every two years

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:47.680
the participants

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:48.548
and answer

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:51.151
lengthy questionnaires every five years

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:52.852
they surrender their medical records.

00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:54.220
Every 15 years

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.456
they are interviewed face to face.

00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:58.725
So what's the life lesson

00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:01.461
Good relationships keep us happier,

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:04.164
healthier and help us live longer.

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:05.832
There's a new book about the study.

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:07.200
It's called The Good Life

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.535
Lessons from the World's

00:01:08.535 --> 00:01:10.537
Longest Scientific Study of Happiness.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.272
The authors are Mark Schultz,

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:13.706
a clinical psychologist,

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:15.708
associate director of the study.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:17.944
And my next guest, Robert Waldinger,

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:18.812
a Harvard

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:20.180
psychiatrist and director

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:21.448
of the Harvard Study

00:01:21.448 --> 00:01:22.849
of Adult Development.

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:23.083
Dr.

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:23.450
Waldinger,

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:24.818
thank you so much for being here.

00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:26.886
Physical fitness, we all know about that.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:28.221
That's important

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.189
but so often

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:30.890
overlooked is what you regard

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.259
as social fitness. What is that

00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:37.530
It's the finding in our research

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.933
that people who do the best,

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:41.968
who are happiest and healthiest,

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:44.137
maintain their relationships.

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:45.105
They're active

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:47.340
in keeping up their relationships,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.175
keeping contact with friends

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:51.277
and family and community members.

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:55.682
You say that the folks who get 50

00:01:56.182 --> 00:01:58.451
are solid in their relationships

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:01.154
are going to be the ones at age 80

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:03.690
who are both physically and mentally.

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:06.559
Most South Is it too late?

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:07.160
I mean, for

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.695
some people watching right now

00:02:08.695 --> 00:02:10.396
who may be older.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:11.131
Is it too late

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:13.600
to follow your prescription?

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.401
It is never too late.

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:16.870
Our study shows us that

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.271
people find friends.

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:19.772
They find love

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:20.740
when they're sure it's

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.042
never going to happen for them at all.

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:24.677
Different points of their lives

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:26.446
And a lot depends on

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:28.681
whether you are intentional

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:31.417
about making and nurturing

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:32.552
good relationships.

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:35.321
Your original pool,

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:37.957
both both sides of the study are

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:39.759
white men.

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:40.827
How reliable

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:41.794
are your findings

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:44.697
for people who are not white men?

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:46.633
Well, now we have gender balance.

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:47.867
So we brought in spouses.

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:49.669
We brought in children,

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:51.371
more than half of whom are women.

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:53.006
So we have gender balance.

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:54.140
And what we do is

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.609
we make sure that our findings

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:57.810
are replicated

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.012
by other studies

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:01.347
of nonwhite people

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:03.683
and by people all over the world.

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:05.518
So we're we are confident

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:07.720
that these findings are true

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.023
for people of all backgrounds.

00:03:11.658 --> 00:03:13.593
I had you on my radio program

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:14.494
and callers,

00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:17.530
after you left, pointed out my deficiency

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:19.933
in not asking about pets

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:22.268
and not asking about religion.

00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:25.271
Address both of those now

00:03:25.772 --> 00:03:26.906
We did not study

00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:28.641
pets, but there are studies

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:30.043
that tell us that

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:32.645
the touch of a pet petting a pet

00:03:32.946 --> 00:03:33.947
actually lowers

00:03:33.947 --> 00:03:34.480
heart rate,

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.115
calms us down,

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:38.117
has a health benefit in that way

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:39.752
with religion.

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:42.755
What we found was that religious people

00:03:42.922 --> 00:03:43.323
weren't

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:45.458
happier than non-religious people

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:47.193
and they weren't less happy.

00:03:47.193 --> 00:03:48.795
But our religious people

00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:50.830
and people who had spiritual practices

00:03:51.164 --> 00:03:54.634
said that they found these practices

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:57.370
a real comfort in times of stress.

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:00.707
There's a lot of data out there.

00:04:00.707 --> 00:04:01.507
Gene Twangy

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.843
comes to mind about the relationship.

00:04:03.843 --> 00:04:05.612
I'm not sure if she would say causation,

00:04:05.612 --> 00:04:07.747
but she would definitely say correlation

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:09.549
between problems of mental

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:11.451
health, anxiety, depression

00:04:11.451 --> 00:04:12.986
among young Americans,

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:15.255
and their connectivity

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:17.590
So how does social media factor

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:18.024
into this?

00:04:18.024 --> 00:04:19.058
Because I'm worried

00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:19.993
that too many young people

00:04:19.993 --> 00:04:21.261
are behind closed doors.

00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:22.462
Not having

00:04:22.462 --> 00:04:23.796
the type of interactions

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:26.466
that you say are so important.

00:04:26.466 --> 00:04:28.134
And it's a big worry right now

00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:29.235
in our culture.

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:31.638
What the research is starting to show us

00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:32.272
is that it

00:04:32.272 --> 00:04:34.774
depends on how we use social media.

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:37.477
If we actively use social media

00:04:37.477 --> 00:04:39.512
to connect with other people,

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:41.481
that's likely to increase

00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:42.849
our happiness levels.

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:45.018
If we passively consume

00:04:45.018 --> 00:04:47.453
somebody else's Instagram feeds

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.690
somebody else's happy pictures of beaches

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:51.658
and parties,

00:04:51.658 --> 00:04:53.726
that's going to lower our self-esteem.

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:54.994
That's going to make us feel like

00:04:54.994 --> 00:04:57.196
we're missing out on the good life,

00:04:57.196 --> 00:04:58.264
which isn't the truth.

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:02.402
You know, time and again in the book,

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:05.138
you would present parallel tracks,

00:05:06.105 --> 00:05:07.740
short bios of some of the folks

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.309
who are in this control group,

00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:10.943
if that's the right way to say it.

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:11.544
The person

00:05:11.544 --> 00:05:13.079
you might expect to end up

00:05:13.079 --> 00:05:15.081
with the happiest life,

00:05:15.081 --> 00:05:17.650
it's often not the case

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:19.018
Yeah.

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:20.486
We found, for example,

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:22.789
we had two people who started out

00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:23.289
as Harvard

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:25.458
undergraduates, lives of privilege,

00:05:25.458 --> 00:05:27.393
all set up to have great lives.

00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:30.129
One was our happiest man

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.032
who had a very ordinary career

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:35.668
as a wonderful high school teacher,

00:05:35.668 --> 00:05:37.403
but nothing fancy.

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:40.173
And another, a very prominent attorney

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:42.608
who worked hard all his life

00:05:43.443 --> 00:05:44.677
and won lots of awards.

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:47.280
And he was one of our least happy people.

00:05:47.647 --> 00:05:48.548
And the difference

00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:49.415
between them

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:50.416
was the extent

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:52.218
to which they paid attention

00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:55.221
to and took care of the connections

00:05:55.221 --> 00:05:58.358
they had with family, friends, community

00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:00.560
Dr.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:01.761
Waldinger, before you leave me,

00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:02.695
give us something

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:05.732
everyone watching can do today

00:06:05.732 --> 00:06:07.834
in line with the findings of your book,

00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:09.235
The Good Life.

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:11.704
Yeah, Take small actions.

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:13.339
It doesn't have to be big.

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:14.540
Think of somebody

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:15.241
you miss,

00:06:15.241 --> 00:06:17.610
somebody you'd like to connect with.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.312
Just send them a text.

00:06:19.312 --> 00:06:21.481
Send them an email saying, Hi,

00:06:21.481 --> 00:06:22.715
I was thinking of you

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:24.951
and just wanted to connect.

00:06:24.951 --> 00:06:27.019
And if you do that regularly

00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:29.222
with the people in your life

00:06:29.222 --> 00:06:30.289
who you value,

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:32.859
you'll be amazed at the positive

00:06:33.993 --> 00:06:36.028
emotion and the positive connection

00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:37.196
that comes back to you.

